Rules, in order of precedence:

1. Carry every prior entry forward **verbatim**. Do not reword, merge or re-order them. The only reasons an
   entry may change are the two below.
2. An entry tagged `[open]` loses the tag, and is rewritten to say what happened, once the member reports back
   on it. If they reported partially, say what they reported and keep the tag.
3. Add one entry per new thing the session established: an event, a preference, a constraint, a struggle, an
   update on something already noted, a topic discussed, or something the counsellor suggested and how the
   member took it.
4. Tag `[open]` any entry recording something the member said they would do and has not yet reported back on.
5. Never add an entry that repeats something already in the notes. An update to an existing fact is a new entry
   only if the fact changed; otherwise leave the original alone.
6. At most {{ max_entries }} entries. If the notes would exceed that, drop the entries that matter least to the
   next session — never an `[open]` one while an untagged one could go instead.

These notes are objective. Every entry says whose account it is:
- What the member told you is the member's account: "Member says…", "Member reports…", "Member believes…".
- Keep the member's own hedges. "I might call her" is "Member is considering calling her", not a plan.
- Another person's thoughts, feelings or motives are only ever the member's reading of them: "Member thinks Dan
  is angry with her", never "Dan is angry with her".
- The counsellor's own reflections, reframes and suggestions are the counsellor's: "Counsellor suggested…".
  Record one as the member's view only if the member clearly took it up, and say so: "Member agreed that…".
- No interpretation of your own: no diagnoses, no labels, no patterns the session did not name.

Every entry is one line, starting with `- `, and is one sentence in the third person, calling the member
"Member". Name the specific thing, the reason and the constraint; vague entries are worthless.

- Good: `- Member says she cannot exercise in the morning because of childcare; evenings are her only window.`
- Bad: `- Member has scheduling issues.`

Terse and dense. Record what was said, committed to or completed, and by whom — not how the session went.
